<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vexin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as French Vexin, Norman Vexin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vexin is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> and the Epte. After 911 it was divided along the Epte into the Norman Vexin, part of Normandy, and the French Vexin around Pontoise, held by the French kings. The frontier was long contested between the dukes of Normandy and the Capetians, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> received his fatal injury during a campaign there in 1087.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vexin.jpg" alt="Vexin" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vexin_localization.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vexin is a plateau on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, crossed by the valleys of the Epte and the Andelle, running roughly from Pontoise to Romilly-sur-Andelle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>. Its name comes from the Gaulish tribe now known as the Veliocasses, whose chief city was Rouen. The French Vexin is a limestone plateau covered with loam, about 40 by 35 kilometres, at 100 to 140 metres, rising to 216 metres at the Buttes de Rosne. Its main road, the Route Nationale 14 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to Rouen, follows a Roman road, the Chaussée Jules César.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The division of 911 came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte">Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</a>, by which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple">Charles the Simple</a> ceded land to the Norman leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>. In 1031 Duke Robert I of Normandy received the French Vexin for helping King Henry I, but in 1082 King Philip I took it back. Both sides fortified the Epte valley, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisors">Gisors</a>, Château-sur-Epte, Trie-Château and La Roche-Guyon, yet the region suffered over a century of pillage. Philip II took Gisors in 1193 and annexed the French Vexin to the royal domain in 1195.</p><p>A time of peace followed: in 1332 Pontoise had 2,150 fiscal hearths. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, which killed 1,000 people in Pontoise in 1348, and the Jacquerie left the land deserted, and the English held the Vexin from 1419 until 1449. During the Wars of Religion it was a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>, and Pontoise opened its gates only after Henry IV abjured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in 1594. In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Pissarro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a>, Cézanne and van Gogh painted its landscapes, and in August 1944 fighting at Fontenay-Saint-Père cost an estimated 2,500 civilian lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old county is shared by five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, Val-d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a>, Yvelines, Oise, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a> and Seine-Maritime, and its chief towns are Pontoise, Vernon, Meulan-en-Yvelines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisors">Gisors</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-andelys">Les Andelys</a>. The plateau remains largely agricultural. Since 1995 most rural communes of the French Vexin have formed the French Vexin Regional Natural Park, headquartered in Théméricourt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vexin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vault stones that fell in 2019 were quarried in the <b>Vexin</b> and presumably brought up the Seine by boat.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Norman Vexin</b>, Henry I of England, duke of Normandy, defeated Louis VI of France, who backed the claim of William Clito.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-20">20 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Veliocasses held the <b>Vexin</b> and the lower Seine valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/veliocasses">Veliocasses</a></li></ul></section>
